            <ArticleTitle>Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation in Pediatric and Cardiac Intensive Care Units.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>961-972</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0031-3955(17)30069-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.pcl.2017.06.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Approximately 5000 to 10,000 children suffer an in-hospital cardiac arrest requiring cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) each year in the United States. Importantly, 2% to 6% of all children admitted to pediatric intensive care units (ICUs) receive CPR, as do 4% to 6% of children admitted to pediatric cardiac ICUs. Survival from pediatric ICU cardiac arrest has improved substantially during the past 20 years presumably due to improved training methods, CPR quality, and post-resuscitation care. Extracorporeal life support CPR remains an important treatment option for both cardiac and noncardiac ICU patients.</AbstractText>
